# C++ standard of the jam project

Ported from the CastleFight and Diskworld standards before the jam start. It holds working conventions only: no
game design. A new rule is added here only by the author's decision.

## Style

- Epic C++ Coding Standard + `.clang-format` in the root: tabs, Allman, braces always, `ColumnLimit: 140`.
  `check_code.py` checks the format, never align by hand.
- Comments and commit messages in English only.
- A comment describes the behaviour that exists, not the intended one. Change the behaviour, fix the comment in the
  same commit.
- Epic prefixes: `b` for bool, `F/U/A/E/I` for types.

## Decomposition

- A decision (who is hit, how much damage, what a rule allows, when the run ends) lives in a pure
  `namespace <Feature>Rules` with no world and no actors: `Public/<System>/<Feature>Rules.h`, free functions with
  `JAMGAME_API`, and a header line `Tests: Jam.<Area>.*`.
- An actor, component or subsystem only glues: collects inputs, calls the rule, applies the result.
- A big class is split into `.cpp` files by responsibility with one shared `.h`.
- Soft thresholds: a `.cpp` over ~600 lines, a function over ~60, nesting over 3. Exceeding one is a signal, not a
  ban: split or justify.

## Systems and dependencies

- Folders `Public/<System>` and `Private/<System>`, plus `Tests`.
- **The simulation does not know about the presentation.** `Core` and `Sim` never include `View`, `UI` or `Audio`.
  The directions are forbidden in the `check_code.py` ratchet (`SIMULATION`, `PRESENTATION`): extend both lists when
  a new system folder appears.
- Balance numbers (prices, health, damage, timings) live in data, not in code.
- Randomness of the simulation comes from a seeded stream, not `FMath::Rand`, so a run can be replayed in a test.

## Logs

- No `LogTemp` in new code. Use the category of the system or `DEFINE_LOG_CATEGORY_STATIC` for a single file.
- Warning and Error only for a breakage or a wrong setup; state changes are `Verbose`, per-tick traces `VeryVerbose`.

## Tests

- Every new decision in `*Rules` gets a test `Jam.<Area>.<Case>` before the code (test first). Glue with the engine
  is checked in PIE, a test is optional.
- The number of `Jam.` tests only grows; a failing test means reverting the change, not bending the test to the code.
- A bug is fixed separately from a refactoring, test first.

## Blueprint seam

- A new Blueprint exposure is opened when needed, not "just in case".
- Logic is not duplicated between an EventGraph and C++: the decision is in C++, the Blueprint only calls.
- Renaming a `UCLASS`/`UPROPERTY`/`UFUNCTION` visible in Blueprints only with CoreRedirects.
- Deletions (C++, assets) only through a dry run with counts and the author's explicit yes.

## Done (end of a task with code)

1. `bash Tools/Build/build_and_test.sh --open` is green: build, `Jam.` tests without failures, checks. The agent
# T-050 The slam grows with each cut

**Block:** session 5 · **Status:** done

## Goal

The author's request (Sat 14 Nov 15:45): the Publisher's slam becomes a pattern that grows with each cut. Before the
first cut, one circle on the hero as now. After the first cut, 3 circles: one around him (no standing close), one
exactly on the hero, one behind the hero that cuts off the way back. From the second cut to the end, 5: the same plus
two more beside the hero. Answers in chat: 1-3-5-5-5; the circle on the hero stays 300 cm, the others are smaller; they
land one after another, 0.2 s apart, from him outwards; a landed cash stack is never covered; the dash still protects.

## Scope

- Simulation, `JamSlamRules`: the circle count by the cuts done, the seeded placement, and the nearest spot the hero
  can walk to in time (walking only: the dash may be stolen). A cutter that would shut every way out is left out; a
  circle over a cash stack is pushed off it. Numbers in `[JamBalance]`.
- The boss puts the whole pattern down in one slam and stands until the last circle lands plus his pause.
- The bot walks to the safe spot while circles are on the floor (the old rule left the first circle only).
- View and sound unchanged: each circle fills on its own clock, each landing flashes, thuds and shakes.

## Test seam

`Jam.Slam.TheCountGrowsWithTheCuts`, `Jam.Slam.ThePatternClosesTheBossTheHeroAndTheWayBack`,
`Jam.Slam.EveryPatternLeavesAReachableSafeSpot`, `Jam.Slam.TheCashStaysClear`, `Jam.Boss.SlamGrowsWithTheCuts`,
`Jam.Bot.WalksOutOfEveryPattern`. Red first on stubs (the four `Jam.Slam` tests); the bot test is red on the old
"leave the circle you stand in" rule (at the wall the bot took two circles, 100 to 32).

The safe-spot sweep uses the game's numbers: hero on a 250 cm grid over the whole arena (wall ring included), the
Publisher at three distances and eight angles, 1, 3 and 5 circles, two seeds: 14808 patterns, none without a spot the
hero reaches walking after 0.3 s, checked by an independent step-by-step walk. All 4936 fives kept their five circles.

## Bot table (20 seeds per policy)

| Policy | T-049 | T-050 |
|---|---|---|
| left | median 101 s | median 104 s |
| right | median 96 s | median 83 s |
| mixed | median 84 s | median 80 s |
| all | 60 of 60, median 92 s, closest win at 43 | 60 of 60, median 83 s (63 to 172), closest win at 23 |

The fight got shorter for the bot: he stands until his last circle lands (up to 0.8 s longer), more openings for the
bot, which never misreads a pattern. A person is expected to lose more health to it; not measured.

## Done

174 `Jam.` tests green, code check without warnings. Clip `Clips/t050_slam_pattern.mp4` (50 s, bot): one circle at
the start, three after the first cut (18 s, 28 s), five after the second (44 s).
#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "Misc/AutomationTest.h"

#if WITH_DEV_AUTOMATION_TESTS

#include "Sim/JamFightOps.h"
#include "Sim/JamStealRules.h"
#include "Tests/JamTestHelpers.h"

/**
 * T-012, T-013: the Publisher's cuts. His health stops at each threshold, he grabs two of the hero's mechanics, the
 * player gives one away on a card while the fight stands still, and the last threshold takes whatever is left.
 * A bug here looks in game like "one big hit skipped a steal", "the card offered the same thing twice", "he took the
 * one I kept", "I died while I was reading the card" or "the key I held for walking picked for me".
 *
 * Run: Automation RunTests Jam.Steal
 */
namespace
{
/** True when the hero still owns the mechanic. */
bool HeroHas(const FJamFight& F, EJamMechanic Mechanic)
{
	return F.Ledger.Has(EJamSide::Hero, Mechanic);
}

/** Steps of the open card with the same answer held. */
void AnswerFor(FJamFight& F, const FJamBalanceData& B, int32 Choice, int32 Steps)
{
	FJamIntents Intents;
	Intents[EJamSide::Hero].Choice = Choice;
	JamTest::Run(F, Intents, B, Steps);
}
} // namespace

IMPLEMENT_SIMPLE_AUTOMATION_TEST(FJamStealClampTest, "Jam.Steal.HealthStopsAtTheThreshold", JamTest::Flags)

bool FJamStealClampTest::RunTest(const FString& Parameters)
{
	const FJamBalanceData B = JamTest::Balance();
	TestEqual(TEXT("the first steal waits at three quarters"), JamStealRules::ThresholdHealth(0, B), 750.f);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the second at a half"), JamStealRules::ThresholdHealth(1, B), 500.f);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the third at a quarter"), JamStealRules::ThresholdHealth(2, B), 250.f);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the final note at a tenth"), JamStealRules::ThresholdHealth(3, B), 100.f);
	TestEqual(TEXT("after the final note nothing holds the health"), JamStealRules::ThresholdHealth(4, B), 0.f);
	TestEqual(TEXT("health above the floor is left alone"), JamStealRules::ClampToThreshold(800.f, 750.f), 800.f);
	TestEqual(TEXT("health below the floor stops at it"), JamStealRules::ClampToThreshold(640.f, 750.f), 750.f);

	FJamFight F = JamTest::Fight(B, FVector2D(-500.0, 0.0), FVector2D::ZeroVector);
	TestEqual(TEXT("a fresh fight holds the boss at the first threshold"), F.Steal.Floor, 750.f);
	F.Fighters[EJamSide::Boss].Health = 760.f;
	JamFightOps::Hurt(F, EJamSide::Boss, 100.f, FVector2D::ZeroVector);
	TestEqual(TEXT("a hit across the threshold stops at it"), F.Fighters[EJamSide::Boss].Health, 750.f);
	JamFightOps::Hurt(F, EJamSide::Hero, 30.f, FVector2D::ZeroVector);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the floor is the boss's, the hero takes the whole hit"), F.Fighters[EJamSide::Hero].Health, 70.f);
	return true;
}

IMPLEMENT_SIMPLE_AUTOMATION_TEST(FJamStealBigHitTest, "Jam.Steal.OneBigHitTriggersOnlyOneSteal", JamTest::Flags)

bool FJamStealBigHitTest::RunTest(const FString& Parameters)
{
	const FJamBalanceData B = JamTest::Balance();
	FJamFight F = JamTest::Fight(B, FVector2D(-500.0, 0.0), FVector2D::ZeroVector);
	JamFightOps::Hurt(F, EJamSide::Boss, 900.f, FVector2D::ZeroVector);
	TestEqual(TEXT("a hit of nine tenths of the bar stops at the first threshold"), F.Fighters[EJamSide::Boss].Health, 750.f);
	JamFightRules::Step(F, FJamIntents(), B);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the step ends with the card up"), F.Phase, EJamPhase::Steal);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the card is announced once"), JamTest::Count(F, EJamEvent::StealOpened), 1);
	TestEqual(TEXT("two mechanics are on the table"), F.Steal.Offer.Num(), 2);

	AnswerFor(F, B, 0, JamSim::Ticks(B.StealChoiceLock));
	TestEqual(TEXT("the answer settles it"), F.Phase, EJamPhase::Fight);
	TestEqual(TEXT("one steal is made"), F.Steal.Done, 1);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the health did not move during it"), F.Fighters[EJamSide::Boss].Health, 750.f);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the next steal waits at a half"), F.Steal.Floor, 500.f);
	JamFightRules::Step(F, FJamIntents(), B);
	TestEqual(TEXT("the fight goes on: the same threshold does not fire twice"), F.Phase, EJamPhase::Fight);
	return true;
}

